bug_content:
Summary: GCC ignores the operation definition of the template
Status: RESOLVED
Severity: normal
Reporter: tangyixuan@mail.dlut.edu.cn
Created: 2020-10-03T12:13:20Z

Description:
Hi, gcc selects the overloaded definition operation+() although it is not declared before.

$ cat s.cpp

#include <iostream>
struct S {
    template <typename T>
    void operator+(T) { std::cout << "1" <<"\n"; }
};
namespace N {
    template <typename T>
    void func(const T value1) { S s; s +(value1); }
}

struct S1 {};
namespace N {
    void operator+(S&, S1) { std::cout << "2" <<"\n"; }
}

int main(int, char**)
{
    S1 s1;
    N::func(s1);
}

$ g++ s.cpp
$ ./a.out
2

$ clang++ s.cpp
$ ./a.out
1

--- Comment #1 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2020-10-05T16:47:23Z ---
Hmm, ICC also produces 2.  Not quite sure what's going on here.

--- Comment #2 by harald@gigawatt.nl at 2020-10-05T23:08:26Z ---
(In reply to Marek Polacek from comment #1)
> Hmm, ICC also produces 2.  Not quite sure what's going on here.

ICC produces 2 by default, but produces 1 when the -strict-ansi command line option is used.

--- Comment #3 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2020-10-07T16:15:43Z ---
Thanks Harald.

Confirmed then, I suppose.

--- Comment #4 by pinskia@gcc.gnu.org at 2021-08-04T17:42:17Z ---
Seems fixed on the trunk.

--- Comment #5 by marxin@gcc.gnu.org at 2021-12-08T14:32:53Z ---
Fixed in r12-702-g6ab1176667734bd6.

--- Comment #6 by pinskia@gcc.gnu.org at 2021-12-08T16:31:08Z ---
Fixed. g++.dg/lookup/operator-3.C covers this issue.

