bug_content:
Summary: Incorrect unused warning for int passed by reference to lambda
Status: RESOLVED
Severity: normal
Reporter: rconde01@hotmail.com
Created: 2018-05-31T17:56:45Z

Description:
https://godbolt.org/g/XLjyWM

When compiled with: -std=c++14 -Wall -Wextra

#include <string>

typedef void(*TheFP)(int & e);

class MyObject
{
public:
    void DoSomething(TheFP)
    {
    }
};

void myFunc()
{
    MyObject m;

    m.DoSomething
    (
        [](auto i)
        {
            i = 5; 
        }
    );
}

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

gives:

In instantiation of 'myFunc()::<lambda(auto:1)> [with auto:1 = int]':

19:18:   required by substitution of 'template<class auto:1> myFunc()::<lambda(auto:1)>::operator decltype (((const myFunc()::<lambda(auto:1)>*)((const myFunc()::<lambda(auto:1)>* const)0))->operator()(static_cast<auto:1&&>(<anonymous>))) (*)(auto:1)() const [with auto:1 = int&]'

23:5:   required from here

19:17: warning: parameter 'i' set but not used [-Wunused-but-set-parameter]

         [](auto i)

            ~~~~~^

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The workaround is to change it to:

void myFunc()
{
    MyObject m;

    m.DoSomething
    (
        [](auto & i)
        {
            i = 5; 
        }
    );
}

