bug_content:
Title: Variable of dependent non-literal type in constexpr not correctly diagnosed
State: open
Labels: c++20, clang:frontend, bugzilla, accepts-invalid, confirmed, constexpr, diverges-from:gcc, diverges-from:edg
Reporter: CaseyCarter
Created: 2020-10-20T20:58:09Z

Description:
|  |  |
| --- | --- |
| Bugzilla Link | [47926](https://llvm.org/bz47926) |
| Version | trunk |
| OS | Windows NT |
| CC | @zygoloid |

## Extended Description 
This TU:
```cpp
    template <class T>
    constexpr bool f() {
        if (!__builtin_is_constant_evaluated()) {
            [[maybe_unused]] T t;
        }

        return true;
    }

    struct nonliteral {
        ~nonliteral() {}
    };

    static_assert(f<nonliteral>(), "should fail");
```
is ill-formed per N4868 [dcl.constexpr]/3.5.3 "The definition of a constexpr function shall satisfy the following requirements:... its function-body shall not enclose... a definition of a variable of non-literal type ...". Clang fails to diagnose this TU (https://godbolt.org/z/xoMGxs).

--- Comment #1 by CaseyCarter at 2020-10-20T22:38:17Z ---
FWIW, I agree with your assessment that this should be considered a defect in the Standard: I thought I was reducing a repro to file an MSVC bug up until the moment I thought to check [dcl.constexpr].

--- Comment #2 by ec04fc15-fa35-46f2-80e1-5d271f2ef708 at 2020-10-20T22:16:53Z ---
I've mailed the CWG and EWG reflectors to ask for opinions here.

Interestingly, MSVC sometimes diagnoses and sometimes doesn't: https://godbolt.org/z/4rPnqz
Looks like some short-circuiting is happening inside MSVC's function template instantiation, and it's somehow only instantiating a 'constexpr portion' of the complete function template specialization?

--- Comment #3 by ec04fc15-fa35-46f2-80e1-5d271f2ef708 at 2020-10-20T22:03:17Z ---
The governing rules are [dcl.constexpr]/7:

> If the instantiated template specialization of a constexpr function template or member function of a class template would fail to satisfy the requirements for a constexpr function, that specialization is still a constexpr function, even though a call to such a function cannot appear in a constant expression. If no specialization of the template would satisfy the requirements for a constexpr function when considered as a non-template function, the template is ill-formed, no diagnostic required.

... and [expr.const]/5.4:


> [An expression is not a constant expression if it would evaluate...]
> an invocation of an instantiated constexpr function that fails to satisfy the requirements for a constexpr function;


So this program isn't ill-formed by [dcl.constexpr]/3.5.3 per se (the instantiation of `f<nonliteral>` itself is valid), but rather is ill-formed because the expression in the static_assert is not a constant expression because it calls a `constexpr` function template specialization that doesn't satisfy the `constexpr` requirements.

In any case, this is an accepts-invalid. We don't actually implement the "if a specialization doesn't satisfy the constexpr requirements then it can't be invoked in a constant expression" rule at all, though we do handle most aspects of it (eg, disallowing virtual function calls and calls to functions with non-literal parameter or return types). In fact, this is likely the only consequence of the rule that we don't handle :)

I'm inclined to say that it's a defect that we still have this rule, especially given P1668R0 and its motivation, and it probably makes sense to retroactively apply this back to C++14 when we got 'if' statements in 'constexpr' functions (and also variable declarations...)

--- Comment #4 by llvmbot at 2025-03-08T15:48:57Z ---

@llvm/issue-subscribers-clang-frontend

Author: Casey Carter (CaseyCarter)

<details>
|  |  |
| --- | --- |
| Bugzilla Link | [47926](https://llvm.org/bz47926) |
| Version | trunk |
| OS | Windows NT |
| CC | @<!-- -->zygoloid |

## Extended Description 
This TU:
```cpp
    template &lt;class T&gt;
    constexpr bool f() {
        if (!__builtin_is_constant_evaluated()) {
            [[maybe_unused]] T t;
        }

        return true;
    }

    struct nonliteral {
        ~nonliteral() {}
    };

    static_assert(f&lt;nonliteral&gt;(), "should fail");
```
is ill-formed per N4868 [dcl.constexpr]/3.5.3 "The definition of a constexpr function shall satisfy the following requirements:... its function-body shall not enclose... a definition of a variable of non-literal type ...". Clang fails to diagnose this TU (https://godbolt.org/z/xoMGxs).
</details>


--- Comment #5 by shafik at 2025-03-14T01:12:03Z ---
clang/MSVC accept gcc/edg reject: https://godbolt.org/z/KeonvMzd9

--- Comment #6 by cor3ntin at 2025-03-14T08:09:20Z ---
Note that this is valid in [C++23](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2022/p2448r2.html) - both the literal requirement AND the requirements on specializations got removed.

P2448 was *NOT* a DR; however unless someone disagrees, I think the status quo of Clang makes sense and
there is no motivation for us to add back that restriction in older language modes

@zygoloid

