bug_content:
Summary: [constexpr] struct with a pointer to one of its members, returned by a function, is not a constant expression
Status: RESOLVED
Severity: normal
Reporter: giu.campana@gmail.com
Created: 2018-01-17T07:17:45Z

Description:
Created attachment 43159
preprocessed source

The following source is rejected by gcc because c_3 is initialized with a non-constant expression

======= source test.cpp ========
struct MyClass
{
    constexpr MyClass(int i_value)
        : m_value(i_value), m_ptr(&m_value)
            { }

    constexpr MyClass(const MyClass & i_source)
        : m_value(i_source.m_value), m_ptr(&m_value)
            { }

    MyClass & operator = (const MyClass & i_source) = delete;

  private:
    int const m_value;
    int const * m_ptr;
};

constexpr auto make_instance(int i_value)
{
    return MyClass(i_value);
}

constexpr auto c_1 = MyClass(34);
const     auto c_2 = make_instance(34);
constexpr auto c_3 = make_instance(34);
==============

Tried with gcc 7.2 and gcc 8.0.1

compiled with:
g++ test.cpp -Wall -Wextra -fsanitize=undefined

output:
test.cpp:27:38: error: ‘MyClass{34, (&<anonymous>.MyClass::m_value)}’ is not a constant expression
 constexpr auto c_3 = make_instance(34);

g++ -v
Using built-in specs.
COLLECT_GCC=/home/abc/gcc_dev_bin/bin/g++
COLLECT_LTO_WRAPPER=/home/abc/gcc_dev_bin/libexec/gcc/x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/8.0.1/lto-wrapper
Target: x86_64-pc-linux-gnu
Configured with: ../gcc_dev/configure --prefix=/home/abc/gcc_dev_bin --disable-multilib
Thread model: posix
gcc version 8.0.1 20180116 (experimental) (GCC)

--- Comment #1 by jakub@gcc.gnu.org at 2018-01-17T14:05:21Z ---
I think for this to work the NRV optimization would need to happen very early so that we see that the c3.m_ptr field is actually &c3.m_value rather than &<temporary>.m_value for a temporary that will be later on NVR optimized to the RESULT_DECL and eventually to c3.  If you do:
void
foo ()
{
  constexpr auto c_4 = make_instance(34);
}
clang++ rejects it too.  So the question is, does the standard mandate NRV to happen here in the c_3 case and when the NRV optimization happens wrt. constexpr evaluation.

--- Comment #2 by jakub@gcc.gnu.org at 2018-01-17T14:39:31Z ---
The reason we don't NRV optimize here is that check_return_expr isn't called with retval of a VAR_DECL, but rather a TARGET_EXPR.  Looking through to TARGET_EXPR to TARGET_EXPR_SLOT ICEs later though.
Anyway, if I change the testcase to:
constexpr auto make_instance(int i_value)
{
    MyClass m(i_value);
    return m;
}
so that it actually is NRV optimized, it still doesn't help and there is still some unrelated temporary created for AGGR_INIT_EXPR rather than the RESULT_DECL mapped to the destination when constexpr evaluating the function call.

--- Comment #3 by giu.campana@gmail.com at 2018-01-18T21:33:55Z ---
The snippet showed by DR2022 (http://open-std.org/JTC1/SC22/WG21/docs/cwg_defects.html#2022) works fine with gcc, but it fails to compile if the object passes through a function. As pointed out by Jakub, in these cases clang succeeds only is the object is not a local constexpr.

--- Comment #4 by pinskia@gcc.gnu.org at 2021-07-23T19:15:52Z ---
Fixed in GCC 10 and above.

--- Comment #5 by pinskia@gcc.gnu.org at 2024-05-01T22:34:28Z ---
Most likely a dup of bug 94470 ...

