bug_content:
Title: modernize-pass-by-value suggest using std::move for types that perform copies on move
State: closed
Labels: clang-tidy, bugzilla
Reporter: 54aefcd4-c07d-4252-8441-723563c8826f
Created: 2016-05-13T13:35:33Z

Description:
|  |  |
| --- | --- |
| Bugzilla Link | [27731](https://llvm.org/bz27731) |
| Resolution | FIXED |
| Resolved on | Aug 29, 2016 02:39 |
| Version | unspecified |
| OS | All |
| CC | @madsravn |

## Extended Description 
In the following

#include<array>
struct A {
  std::array<10, int> a_;
  A(std::array<10, int> a) : a_(a) {}
};

clang-tidy's modernize-pass-by-value check suggests writing

#include<array>
struct A {
  std::array<10, int> a_;
  A(std::array<10, int> a) : a_(std::move(a)) {}
};


instead even though the elements of the array will be copied (the array storage cannot be moved and moving an `int` is the same as copying an `int`). 

The second form (using `std::move`) is actually worse since it suggests that a particular constructor is cheap when it isn't, consider:

using ints_t = std::array<4096, int>;

struct A {
  ints_t a_;
  A(ints_t a) : a_(std::move(a)) {} // looks fast, is slow
};

--- Comment #1 by madsravn at 2016-08-29T09:39:26Z ---
Has been fixed: https://reviews.llvm.org/D20365

--- Comment #2 by 54aefcd4-c07d-4252-8441-723563c8826f at 2016-05-13T16:26:10Z ---
In particular, modernize-pass-by-value tells the user to use `std::move()` in the previous example, but when one does so then the check "misc-move-const-arg" complains that:

> std::move of the variable of trivially-copyable type has no effect

This is cyclic.

