}
============================================================

--- Comment #12 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-23T13:05:49Z ---
What's changed in r231197 is that previously we had
return <retval> = (int) ((long int) ((char *) &a + (sizetype) q) - (long int) &a.c);
but now
return <retval> = (int) ((long int) ((char *) &a + 18446744073709551615) - (long int) &a.c);

--- Comment #13 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-23T14:44:22Z ---
Uh.  (sizetype) q is folded to -1 in constant_value_1.  DECL_INITIAL of "q" is -1, that's what build_zero_init_1 did:
 177   else if (TYPE_PTR_OR_PTRMEM_P (type))
 178     init = fold (convert (type, nullptr_node));
type is offset_type.  Why is nullptr_node converted to offset_type -1?!

--- Comment #14 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-23T14:54:56Z ---
Ah, I see cp_convert_to_pointer does

 212       /* A NULL pointer-to-data-member is represented by -1, not by
 213          zero.  */
 214       tree val = (TYPE_PTRDATAMEM_P (type)
 215                   ? build_int_cst_type (type, -1)
 216                   : build_int_cst (type, 0));

--- Comment #15 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-23T16:56:32Z ---
Slightly reduced original testcase:

struct A
{
  char c;
};

int main()
{
  static char A::* const q = nullptr;
  A a;
  return &(a.*q) - &a.c;
}

--- Comment #16 by reichelt@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-23T18:30:44Z ---
According to [expr.mptr.oper] the code in comment 15 has undefined behavior, because the second operand of ->* is the null-pointer-to-member-value.

--- Comment #17 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-23T18:47:54Z ---
I see, thanks for pointing that out.

--- Comment #18 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-23T22:28:51Z ---
An alternative, and hopefully still valid testcase:

struct A
{
  char c;
};

int main()
{
  static char A::* q;
  A a;
  return &(a.*q) - &a.c;
}

--- Comment #19 by reichelt@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-24T09:33:59Z ---
No, that's again invalid.
The second operand of .* or .-> must point to a valid class member,
otherwise you'll get undefined behavior.
The only way to achieve this in this class with a single member is to
initialize it with &A::c (or a copy of this value).

--- Comment #20 by mpolacek@gcc.gnu.org at 2017-02-24T12:08:21Z ---
Oh well.  Anyway, this

