bug_content:
Summary: missing check for destructibility of base classes in aggregate initialization
Status: RESOLVED
Severity: normal
Reporter: richard-gccbugzilla@metafoo.co.uk
Created: 2020-08-31T20:53:08Z

Description:
GCC accepts this invalid code (which is ill-formed because [dcl.init.aggr]/8 says it potentially-invokes the destructor for B, which [class.dtor]/15 says requires the destructor to be accessible):

struct B {
protected:
    ~B() {}
};

struct C : B { int n; };

int f();

void g() {
    C c{{}, f()};
}

... and generates wrong code for this similar example:

#include <stdio.h>

struct B {
public:
    ~B() { puts("destroyed"); }
};

struct C : B { int n; };

int f() { throw "hello"; }

int main() {
    try {
    C c{{}, f()};
    } catch (const char*) {
    }
}

... which is required to print "destroyed" (when the B base class subobject is destroyed during stack unwinding), but with GCC does not.

