bug_content:
Title: typeof rejects initialiser expression
State: open
Labels: clang:frontend, bugzilla
Reporter: llvmbot
Created: 2015-10-25T20:55:29Z

Description:
|  |  |
| --- | --- |
| Bugzilla Link | [25315](https://llvm.org/bz25315) |
| Version | 3.7 |
| OS | Linux |
| Reporter | LLVM Bugzilla Contributor |
| CC | @hfinkel |

## Extended Description 
The following code:

```c
extern void *blah(void *arg);

const void *test2(const void *arg)
{
    return blah( ( {
        union {
            typeof((*(const void * []) { (arg) })) _value;
            void * _cast;
        } _union;
        _union._value = ((*(const void * []) { (arg) }));
        _union._cast;
    } ) );
}
```

generates the error:

```
test.c:7:43: error: initializer element is not a compile-time constant
            typeof((*(const void * []) { (arg) })) _value;
                                          ^~~
1 error generated.
```

This code compiles just fine with gcc, and if the typeof(...) expression is replaced (with const void *) clang compiles this ok as well.

By the way, bug #&#8203;11299 (#11671) looks related, but I can't say if it really is.

--- Comment #1 by llvmbot at 2017-07-02T19:00:42Z ---
Because this bug appears to have been ignored for nearly two years, I thought I might post a slightly more compelling use case:

```c
#define CAST_FROM_TO(from_type, to_type, value) \
    ( { \
        union { \
            from_type _value; \
            to_type _cast; \
        } _union = { ._value = (value) }; \
        _union._cast; \
    } )
#define CAST_TO(to_type, value) CAST_FROM_TO(typeof(value), to_type, value)


struct idelay {
    unsigned int value : 5;
};

void hw_write_rev_clk_idelay(struct idelay *idelay, unsigned int delay)
{
    unsigned int temp_val = CAST_TO(unsigned int,
         ((struct idelay) { .value = delay & 0x1F } ));
    unsigned int *temp_reg = (unsigned int *) idelay;
    *temp_reg = temp_val;
}
```

With clang 3.8.1 this fails to compile, but if the code is written out and the typeofs are elaborated it compiles.

This sort of painful dance can be required for hardware addressing.

--- Comment #2 by xiver77 at 2022-06-11T07:05:09Z ---
5 years later with Clang ver14, the bug is still not fixed. Below is basically the same use case as the last comment, a bit simplified.

```
#define REINT(T, X) (union {__typeof__(X) x; T t;}){X}.t

int f2i(float x) {
  return REINT(int, x);
}

float i2f(int x) {
  return REINT(float, x);
}

float f2i2f(float x) {
  return REINT(float, REINT(int, x));
}
```

`f2i` and `i2f` compiles fine, but the last one gets rejected.

