bug_content:
Summary: [10 Regression] C++17 wrongly reports aggregate type as not-aggregate (when explicitly defaulted ctors are added)
Status: RESOLVED
Severity: normal
Reporter: igor.chorazewicz@intel.com
Created: 2019-12-09T18:11:33Z

Description:
The following code, when compiled with -std=c++17 results in "0" being printed. The correct behavior is to print "1". Removing ctors fixes the issue, however, accordingly to the standard, explicitly defaulted ctors are allowed for an aggregate.

#include <array>
#include <type_traits>
#include <iostream>

template <typename T, std::size_t N>
struct A {
    A() = default;
    A(const A &) = default;
    A(A &&) = default;

    T arr[N];   
};

int main()
{
    std::cout << std::is_aggregate<A<int,3>>::value;
}

--- Comment #1 by pinskia@gcc.gnu.org at 2019-12-09T18:22:09Z ---
Most likely does not implement the difference between C++11/14 and C++17 with respect to default member initializers.

--- Comment #2 by igor.chorazewicz@intel.com at 2019-12-10T09:18:26Z ---
Hm, from what I thought C++11 (not sure about C++14 but probably also) allows ctors to be explicitly defaulted for aggregates. See C++11 [dcl.init.aggr]. 

Consider the following code:

#include <array>
#include <type_traits>
#include <iostream>

template <typename T, std::size_t N>
struct A {
    A() = default;
    A(const A &) = default;
    A(A &&) = default;

    T arr[N];   
};

int main()
{
    A<int, 3> a {{1,2,3}};
}

This code compiles with g++ 9.2 for -std=c++11, -std=c++14, std=c++17 (but not for -std=c++2a) and fails to compile for g++ 10 for all standards

--- Comment #3 by jakub@gcc.gnu.org at 2019-12-10T12:50:29Z ---
The #c2 testcase is rejected since r278298 aka P1816R0 implementation.

--- Comment #4 by jakub@gcc.gnu.org at 2019-12-10T12:54:11Z ---
And the
template <typename T, __SIZE_TYPE__ N>
struct A {
  A() = default;
  A(const A &) = default;
  A(A &&) = default;
  T arr[N];   
};

int main()
{
  if (!__is_aggregate (A<int,3>))
    __builtin_abort ();
}
testcase reduced from #c0 too.

--- Comment #5 by igor.chorazewicz@intel.com at 2019-12-10T13:03:39Z ---
Ok, but P1816R0 talks about argument deduction - in my example I specify all template arguments for A, so should this fail?

Moreover, for g++ 10 and g++ 9.2 even the following, non-template code fails for -std=c++2a

struct A {
    A() = default;
    A(const A &) = default;
    A(A &&) = default;

    int arr[3];   
};

int main()
{
    A a = {{1,2,3}};
}

--- Comment #6 by jakub@gcc.gnu.org at 2019-12-10T13:11:55Z ---
#c5 is rejected with -std=c++2a starting with r263115 aka http://wg21.link/p1008r1
So, I believe it is correct to reject this in C++20, but I think neither of those papers have been a DR and so shouldn't affect C++17 or earlier compilation modes.

--- Comment #7 by jakub@gcc.gnu.org at 2019-12-10T13:28:05Z ---
Created attachment 47458
gcc10-pr92869.patch

Thus, I think we want to do something like this.  Untested so far.

