bug_content:
Title: -Wpacked warns even though packed structure has different size/memory layout
State: open
Labels: bugzilla, clang:diagnostics
Reporter: db3989e7-7410-4315-9bfe-828e64d86539
Created: 2013-12-04T15:23:21Z

Description:
|  |  |
| --- | --- |
| Bugzilla Link | [18132](https://llvm.org/bz18132) |
| Version | 3.3 |
| OS | All |
| CC | @behdad |

## Extended Description 
Consider the following rather simple code snippet:

----- packed.c ----
```cpp
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>

struct S1 {
	uint16_t addr;
	uint32_t value;
};

struct S2 {
	uint16_t addr;
	uint32_t value;
} __attribute__((packed));


int main (int argc, char ** argv) {
	struct S1 s1;
	struct S2 s2;

	printf("%zu - %zu\n", sizeof(s1), sizeof(s2));
	return 0;
};
```

Compiling and running this code should produce the following output on a 32/64 bit system:

8 - 6

(I think it may be 6 - 6 on a 16 bit system)

So whether the struct is packed or not *DOES* make a difference here. Yet, compiling the code above using "-Wpacked", I get the following:
```
packed.c:10:11: warning: packed attribute is unnecessary for 'addr' [-Wpacked]
        uint16_t addr;
                 ^
```
This doesn't seem correct to me. As I understand "-Wpacked", it should only produce a warning if the presence of the packed attribute has no influence on the structure size and thus also no influence on the relative memory layout of the fields within the structure.

--- Comment #1 by behdad at 2018-11-22T03:33:06Z ---
I have a more fundamental problem with `-Wpacked`.  My struct is trivial:
```cpp
struct __attribute__((packed)) packed_int 
{ 
  int i; 
}; 
```
This produces two warnings:
```
a.c:10:7: warning: packed attribute is unnecessary for 'i' [-Wpacked]
  int i;
      ^
a.c:8:32: warning: packed attribute is unnecessary for 'struct packed_int' [-Wpacked]
struct __attribute__((packed)) packed_int
```
However, this is a legitimate and necessary use of the attribute.  The resulting struct has different alignment than without the attribute (or is that not guaranteed by the compiler, unless I also add a "char c" to the struct?).

Indeed, with the following code:

```cpp
static struct X 
{ 
  char a; 
  char b[2]; 
} x; 
 
struct __attribute__((packed)) packed_int 
{ 
  int i; 
}; 
 
int 
main (void) 
{ 
  return ((struct packed_int *) &x.b)->i; 
} 
```
I the above two warnings are the only I get.  If I remove the attribute however, I get the following:
```
a.c:16:11: warning: cast from 'char (*)[2]' to 'struct packed_int *' increases required alignment from 1
      to 4 [-Wcast-align]
  return ((struct packed_int *) &x.b)->i;
          ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 warning generated.
```
which is, indeed what I was trying to avoid.

Worse, I cannot just skip defining the struct and use a typedef or put the attribute in the cast.  Neither works:
```cpp
static struct X 
{ 
  char a; 
  char b[2]; 
} x; 
 
typedef __attribute__((packed)) int packed_int; 
 
int 
main (void) 
{ 
  return * ((packed_int *) &x.b); 
} 
```
will result in:
```
$ clang a.c -Weverything
a.c:8:24: warning: 'packed' attribute ignored [-Wignored-attributes]
typedef __attribute__((packed)) int packed_int;
                       ^
a.c:13:13: warning: cast from 'char (*)[2]' to 'packed_int *' (aka 'int *') increases required alignment
      from 1 to 4 [-Wcast-align]
  return * ((packed_int *) &x.b);
            ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
```
and:
```cpp
static struct X 
{ 
  char a; 
  char b[2]; 
} x; 
 
int 
main (void) 
{ 
  return * ((__attribute__((packed)) int *) &x.b); 
} 
```
same:
```
behdad:~ 0$ clang a.c -Weverything
a.c:11:29: warning: 'packed' attribute ignored when parsing type [-Wignored-attributes]
  return * ((__attribute__((packed)) int *) &x.b);
                            ^~~~~~
a.c:11:13: warning: cast from 'char (*)[2]' to 'int *' increases required alignment from 1 to 4
      [-Wcast-align]
  return * ((__attribute__((packed)) int *) &x.b);
            ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 warnings generated.
```

So, the packed on the struct is necessary.

Even if I add a char to the struct, clang still warns about the int part:

```cpp
static struct X 
{ 
  char a; 
  char b[2]; 
} x; 
 
struct __attribute__((packed)) packed_int 
{ 
  int i; 
  char c; 
}; 
 
int 
main (void) 
{ 
  return ((struct packed_int *) &x.b)->i; 
} 
```
```
behdad:~ 130$ clang a.c -Weverything
a.c:10:7: warning: packed attribute is unnecessary for 'i' [-Wpacked]
  int i;
      ^
1 warning generated.
```

This just doesn't make sense.

