| CC | @rnk,@timurrrr |

## Extended Description 
consider:
struct A {
  virtual void fun() {}
};
struct B : A {
  virtual void fun() {}
};
struct C : A, B {
  virtual void gun() {}
};
struct D : B, virtual C {
  D() {}
  virtual void gun() {}
};

D d;

.obj: offset 0 in vftable ??_7D@@6BB@@@ has mismatch: ?fun@B@@UAEXXZ vs ?fun@B@@$4PPPPPPPI@A@AEXXZ

--- Comment #1 by timurrrr at 2014-04-23T11:02:51Z ---
r206908.

--- Comment #2 by timurrrr at 2014-04-22T13:32:34Z ---
http://reviews.llvm.org/D3449

--- Comment #3 by timurrrr at 2014-04-22T12:09:27Z ---
This one's funny - we have two B::fun final overriders present in the D class.
ComputeBaseOffset() call done in CalculateVtordispAdjustment() returns the D's direct nvbase B as the final overrider.

The vtordisp adjustment is redundant here indeed -- I'll take a look.

--- Comment #4 by 991901f3-cc14-4404-b340-165691b62a58 at 2014-04-22T05:24:38Z ---
assigned to @timurrrr

patch_content:
Commit Message:
Implement DR990 and DR1070. Aggregate initialization initializes uninitialized
elements from {}, rather than value-initializing them. This permits calling an
initializer-list constructor or constructing a std::initializer_list object.
(It would also permit initializing a const reference or rvalue reference if
that weren't explicitly prohibited by other rules.)

llvm-svn: 210091

Author: Richard Smith
Date: 2014-06-03T08:26:00Z
