bug_content:
Title: Assertion failure with GVNHoist: Assertion `MSSA->dominates(NewDef, FirstDef) && "Should have dominated the new access"' failed.
State: closed
Labels: bugzilla
Reporter: efriedma-quic
Created: 2018-02-23T02:44:22Z

Description:
|  |  |
| --- | --- |
| Bugzilla Link | [36484](https://llvm.org/bz36484) |
| Resolution | FIXED |
| Resolved on | May 18, 2018 10:36 |
| Version | trunk |
| OS | Windows NT |
| CC | @hiraditya,@nikic,@JonPsson,@Vsevolod-Livinskij |

## Extended Description 
Testcase (crashes with "opt -gvn-hoist"):

target datalayout = "e-m:e-p:32:32-i64:64-v128:64:128-a:0:32-n32-S64"

define void @&#8203;f(i8* %p) {
entry:
  switch i4 undef, label %if.then30 [
    i4 4, label %if.end
    i4 0, label %if.end
  ]

if.end:
  br label %if.end19

if.end19:
  br i1 undef, label %e, label %e.thread

e.thread:
  store i8 0, i8* %p, align 4
  br label %if.then30

if.then30:
  call void @&#8203;g()
  unreachable

e:
  store i8 0, i8* %p, align 4
  unreachable
}

declare void @&#8203;g()



--------------------------------------------------------------------

The issue is related to the MemoryPhi in the block if.then30.  MemorySSAUpdater::moveTo erases the store in the block e.thread, then erases the MemoryPHI in if.then30 because it's trivial (temporarily), then tries to insert a def in if.end19.  fixupDefs then asserts that if.end19 dominates if.then30, which is not true.

Not sure what the right fix is here.

--- Comment #1 by nikic at 2018-05-18T10:11:33Z ---
The mentioned revision landed and both attached test cases no longer assert, so this can probably be closed.

--- Comment #2 by JonPsson at 2018-03-12T14:55:30Z ---
[reduced SystemZ testcase](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/55128014/143757102-3fd58144-53e1-4b28-852d-e86d0f43b13c.gz)
The same assert triggered in SystemZ testing also:

opt -O3 -S -mtriple=s390x-linux-gnu -mcpu=z13 -o /dev/null -enable-gvn-hoist -enable-loopinterchange -enable-newgvn -enable-tbaa=false ./tc_memssadom.ll

MemorySSAUpdater.cpp:339: void llvm::MemorySSAUpdater::fixupDefs(const llvm::SmallVectorImpl<llvm::MemoryAccess*>&): Assertion `MSSA->dominates(NewDef, FirstDef) && "Should have dominated the new access"' failed.
...

--- Comment #3 by llvmbot at 2018-03-09T18:41:10Z ---
https://reviews.llvm.org/D44257

Attempt to fix it by first approach suggested by Daniel.

--- Comment #4 by llvmbot at 2018-02-23T04:30:04Z ---
The bug is in determining the phi is trivial.
It should have detected the definition as a new operand.
Reasoning:

If the values from all incoming preds to a phi are the same, by definition that value must dominate any users of the phi. The only other possibility is the dominator tree is incorrect. It can't be possible for the value to reach it on every pred and the value not be in a dominating block, because the that's the definition of dominance.

Thus, the logic about same operands is correct.

This leaves the other possibility: we are detecting it as trivial when it isn't.

In fact, this phi is not trivial, because the def we are inserting is an operand, and thus it merges live on entry and the new def.

The proximate cause of this bug is that, as you allude to, tryRemoveTrivialPhi will search downwards in some cases, but fixupDefs has not occurred yet, so those defs are not guaranteed to be correct.

The smallest hammer i can think of that will solve this problem is, during moves, mark the direct users of the erased things that were phis as "not to be optimized".  This should prevent this from occurring.

The other thing to do would be to fix this code:
// Recurse over a set of phi uses to eliminate the trivial ones
MemoryAccess *MemorySSAUpdater::recursePhi(MemoryAccess *Phi) {
  if (!Phi)
    return nullptr;
  TrackingVH<MemoryAccess> Res(Phi);
  SmallVector<TrackingVH<Value>, 8> Uses;
  std::copy(Phi->user_begin(), Phi->user_end(), std::back_inserter(Uses));
  for (auto &U : Uses) {
    if (MemoryPhi *UsePhi = dyn_cast<MemoryPhi>(&*U)) {
      auto OperRange = UsePhi->operands();
      tryRemoveTrivialPhi(UsePhi, OperRange);
    }
  }
  return Res;
}


If this is changed to use getPreviousDefFromEnd for each pred, and form a temporary operand list of those answers, instead of the "using the current set of phi operands" it should get the correct answer.

(because it will return the def that should be the phi operand, instead of what currently is there temporarily until fixup occurs)


This appears to be a bug in the paper when used as an update algorithm.

It does the right thing during phi insertion (it reads the ops from the predecessor), but not during phi optimization.  This can't go wrong during construction, but can go wrong during updating.

We could probably get away with only re-discovering the operands for those phis touched by our previous erase operation (IE a combination of both changes), but i don't have time to prove it to myself ATM.

(Here's a patch for the second idea, tested on this but otherwise untested. I'm worried it may be tugging at a string that will unravel, so it may be easier to just not try to optimize them:

diff --git a/lib/Analysis/MemorySSAUpdater.cpp b/lib/Analysis/MemorySSAUpdater.cpp
index f5d89f699a5..536f89b5508 100644
--- a/lib/Analysis/MemorySSAUpdater.cpp
+++ b/lib/Analysis/MemorySSAUpdater.cpp
@@ -150,8 +150,14 @@ MemoryAccess *MemorySSAUpdater::recursePhi(MemoryAccess *Phi) {
   std::copy(Phi->user_begin(), Phi->user_end(), std::back_inserter(Uses));
   for (auto &U : Uses) {
     if (MemoryPhi *UsePhi = dyn_cast<MemoryPhi>(&*U)) {
-      auto OperRange = UsePhi->operands();
-      tryRemoveTrivialPhi(UsePhi, OperRange);
+      // To correct do optimization in the middle of updating, in particular,
+      // while moving operands, we must always read the operands for phis that
+      // were modifying by the erase operation.  We don't track that ATM, so
+      // instead we always re-read operands.
+      SmallVector<MemoryAccess *, 8> PhiOps;
+      for (auto *Pred : predecessors(UsePhi->getBlock()))
+        PhiOps.push_back(getPreviousDefFromEnd(Pred));
+      tryRemoveTrivialPhi(UsePhi, PhiOps);
     }
   }
   return Res;

)

