bug_content:
Title: AArch64 fails to select the correct addp
State: closed
Labels: backend:AArch64, bugzilla
Reporter: 4c958a03-4ad7-407c-a85b-320d9a7221b0
Created: 2019-03-05T19:02:15Z

Description:
|  |  |
| --- | --- |
| Bugzilla Link | [40968](https://llvm.org/bz40968) |
| Resolution | FIXED |
| Resolved on | Mar 21, 2019 15:44 |
| Version | trunk |
| OS | Linux |
| CC | @aemerson,@Arnaud-de-Grandmaison-ARM,@ornata,@smithp35,@TNorthover |

## Extended Description 
This code:

typedef __attribute__((neon_vector_type(2))) float float32x2_t;
int printf(const char *, ...);
static __inline__ __attribute__((__always_inline__, __nodebug__)) float32x2_t vcreate_f32(unsigned long __p0) {
  float32x2_t __ret;
  __ret = (float32x2_t)(__p0);
  return __ret;
}
int main() {
  float32x2_t c;
  float32x2_t d;
  float32x2_t e;
  c = vcreate_f32(0x3f69925b3e663ec8UL);
  d = vcreate_f32(0x3f4375073f409923UL);
  e = __builtin_neon_vpadd_v(d, c, 9);
  float f =  __builtin_neon_vget_lane_f32(e, 0);
  printf("%g\n", f);
}

Should print ~1.51584. It seems to currently print "8.77658e+37".


This started to fail at -O0 (with global isel) after rL355344, but I think that's just because it no longer falls back to isel. It appears to be selecting ADDP (i32x2), not FADDP (f32x2).

--- Comment #1 by aemerson at 2019-03-21T22:44:41Z ---
The temporary workaround is removed as of r356722 and replaced with a proper solution of splitting the addp intrinsic into int and fp variants.

--- Comment #2 by ornata at 2019-03-11T20:53:58Z ---
I committed a workaround:
https://reviews.llvm.org/rL355865

This isn't really "solved" though since the workaround is just to always fall back to SelectionDAG when we run into this.

--- Comment #3 by 4c958a03-4ad7-407c-a85b-320d9a7221b0 at 2019-03-06T12:54:23Z ---
Yep, I agree. Making this fail without that patch just requires pulling the __builtin_neon_vpadd_v into a separate function:

typedef __attribute__((neon_vector_type(2))) float float32x2_t;
int printf(const char *, ...);
static __inline__ __attribute__((__always_inline__, __nodebug__)) float32x2_t vcreate_f32(unsigned long __p0) {
  float32x2_t __ret;
  __ret = (float32x2_t)(__p0);
  return __ret;
}
float32x2_t test(float32x2_t d, float32x2_t c) {
  return __builtin_neon_vpadd_v(d, c, 9);
}
int main() {
  float32x2_t c;
  float32x2_t d;
  float32x2_t e;
  c = vcreate_f32(0x3f69925b3e663ec8UL);
  d = vcreate_f32(0x3f4375073f409923UL);
  e = test(d,c);
  float f =  __builtin_neon_vget_lane_f32(e, 0);
  printf("%g\n", f);
}

--- Comment #4 by ornata at 2019-03-05T21:12:02Z ---
Yeah, this isn't actually caused by r355344.

This is actually that we don't have the information necessary to know that we need a faddp rather than an addp, so when we pattern match, we end up with the wrong add.


For example, we start out with, say, this:

%vpadd_v3 = call <2 x float> @&#8203;llvm.aarch64.neon.addp.v2f32(<2 x float> %vpadd_v, <2 x float> %vpadd_v2)


Which will be translated into this:

%22:fpr(<2 x s32>) = G_INTRINSIC intrinsic(@llvm.aarch64.neon.addp), %20:fpr(<2 x s32>), %21:fpr(<2 x s32>)


At this point we've entirely dropped the fact that we're working on vectors of *floats*. So, it's selected into this:

%22:fpr64(<2 x s32>) = ADDPv2i32 %20:fpr64(<2 x s32>), %21:fpr64(<2 x s32>)


Which is incorrect. So, we have a bit deeper of a problem here.

