bug_content:
Title: Buggy mlir::interchangeLoops (ArrayRef<AffineForOp>,ArrayRef<unsigned>)
State: closed
Labels: bugzilla, mlir:affine
Reporter: llvmbot
Created: 2020-03-27T12:51:00Z

Description:
|  |  |
| --- | --- |
| Bugzilla Link | [45328](https://llvm.org/bz45328) |
| Resolution | FIXED |
| Resolved on | Apr 02, 2020 11:03 |
| Version | unspecified |
| OS | All |
| Reporter | LLVM Bugzilla Contributor |
| CC | @joker-eph,@bondhugula |
| Fixed by commit(s) | f273e5c50745d8496846688e3019bc8ef68004e4 |

## Extended Description 
The function mlir::interchangeLoops (ArrayRef<AffineForOp>,ArrayRef<unsigned>) of file mlir/lib/Transforms/Utils/LoopUtils.cpp performs an incorrect action for most (but not all) permutations provided as input.

For instance, assume the loop nest is:

    affine.for %arg0 = 0 to 100 {     // outer loop
      affine.for %arg1 = 0 to 300 {   // middle loop
        affine.for %arg2 = 0 to 200 { // inner loop

Then, I perform the interchange using:

    SmallVector<unsigned,3> index_map ;
    SmallVector<AffineForOp,3> loop_nest ;
    index_map.push_back(2) ;
    index_map.push_back(1) ;
    index_map.push_back(0) ;
    loop_nest.push_back(outer_loop) ;
    loop_nest.push_back(middle_loop) ;
    loop_nest.push_back(inner_loop) ;
    mlir::interchangeLoops(loop_nest,index_map) ;

In the end I get the loop nest:

    affine.for %arg0 = 0 to 300 {
      affine.for %arg1 = 0 to 200 {
        affine.for %arg2 = 0 to 100 {

The permutation is quite different from what I expected, which is:

    affine.for %arg0 = 0 to 200 {    // original index 2 goes to 0
      affine.for %arg1 = 0 to 300 {  // original index 1 goes to 1
        affine.for %arg2 = 0 to 100 {// original index 0 goes to 2

The bug is in the implementation of the function interchangeLoop.
For my permutation, the code will only sink by 2 for i==0, which gives exactly the result provided by the code, but not the good permutation.
Formally, the code decomposes the permutation into rotations (sinkLoop), but the code is incorrect because it does not update the loopPermMap while rotations are performed, hence the bug.

Here is an implementation of the buggy function that should work. 
It's not very efficient, but this bit of code is not performance-critical.
And it has the advantage of being rather clear.

/// Performs a sequence of loop interchanges of loops in perfectly nested
/// sequence of loops in 'loops', as specified by permutation in 'loopPermMap'.
unsigned mlir::interchangeLoops(ArrayRef<AffineForOp> loops,
                                ArrayRef<unsigned> loopPermMap) {
  // 
  Optional<unsigned> loopNestRootIndex;  
  // Store the current depth of each affine loop in the
  // nest. Initially, loop i is at depth i.
  unsigned nest_depth = loops.size() ;
  unsigned curr[nest_depth] ;
  for (unsigned i = 0; i<nest_depth; i++)
    curr[i] = i ;

  // Each iteration will make sure that the loop k with
  // loopPermMap[k]=depth arrives at its place, for
  // depth=(nest_depth-1) downto 0.
  for(unsigned depth=nest_depth; depth > 0 ;) {
    depth-- ;

    // Find the loop k with loopPermMap[k]=depth
    unsigned k = 0 ;
    while((loopPermMap[k]!=depth)&&(k<nest_depth))k++ ;
    assert(k < nest_depth) ;
    if(depth==0)loopNestRootIndex = k ; // Save it for later    
    // Given the way the construction is done, the following
    // assertion should hold. 
    assert(curr[k]<=depth) ;
    // If loop k is not already at its place, I have to sink it
    // and update the curr data structure.
    if(depth>curr[k]) {
      sinkLoop(loops[k], depth-curr[k]);
      for(unsigned int i=0;i<nest_depth;i++) {
	if((curr[k]<curr[i])&&(curr[i]<=depth)) curr[i]-- ;
      }
      curr[k] = depth ;
    }
  }
  assert(loopNestRootIndex.hasValue());
  return loopNestRootIndex.getValue();
}

--- Comment #1 by bondhugula at 2020-03-29T18:34:42Z ---
This pending patch https://reviews.llvm.org/D77004 fixes this issue.

--- Comment #2 by bondhugula at 2020-03-28T01:29:17Z ---
Instead of fixing this approach and continuing to use sinkLoops (which would be inefficient/unnecessary work in this case), it's just better to rewrite this cleanly.

--- Comment #3 by llvmbot at 2020-03-27T12:51:00Z ---
assigned to @bondhugula

